    "data_quality_notes": "Comprehensive data compiled from primary databases. UniProt entry Q9UP38 is reviewed (Swiss-Prot quality). High-quality cryo-EM structures available for both active (Gq-coupled, PDB 8J9N, 3.6Å) and inactive (apo, PDB 8J9O, 3.4Å) states. WNT3A binding affinity well-characterized with Kd ~3.5-12.5 nM depending on experimental conditions. DVL interaction through PDZ-binding motif well-documented but quantitative Kd values not available. N-glycosylation and O-glycosylation sites are predicted based on sequon motifs and require experimental validation. Disulfide bond pattern inferred from family conservation and structural studies of related FZD receptors. TM helix boundaries estimated from structural studies and sequence alignments. LRP5/6 co-receptor interaction is WNT-dependent and indirect. AlphaFold structure prediction shows high confidence for structured domains (CRD, 7TM) and lower confidence for flexible regions (loops, termini).",
    "modeling_relevance_summary": "FZD1 is an excellent candidate for rule-based modeling of canonical Wnt signaling pathway. Key features for modeling: (1) Well-characterized ligand binding with quantitative Kd values for WNT3A (3.5 nM), WNT3 (15 nM), and WNT1 (20 nM); (2) Ternary complex formation with WNT and LRP5/6 co-receptors representing cooperative binding; (3) Multiple DVL recruitment through PDZ-binding motif enabling signal amplification; (4) GPCR coupling to Gq protein with structural evidence (PDB 8J9N); (5) Post-translational modifications (N-glycosylation, O-glycosylation, phosphorylation, palmitoylation) providing regulatory mechanisms; (6) Distinct active/inactive conformations captured by cryo-EM structures; (7) Integration point for canonical and non-canonical Wnt pathways; (8) Expression across multiple tissues enabling tissue-specific modeling. Main challenges: DVL binding kinetics not quantitatively characterized; LRP5/6 co-receptor interaction is complex and WNT-dependent; receptor oligomerization state unclear; endocytosis and trafficking dynamics not well-defined; relative contributions of canonical vs. non-canonical signaling difficult to parse. The 10 conserved cysteines forming 5 disulfide bonds in CRD domain are critical for proper folding and WNT recognition. The KTxxxW motif (KQRDDYS in FZD1, residues 601-607) is essential for DVL recruitment and downstream signaling. Disease associations (cancer, osteoporosis) provide validation opportunities for pathway models."
  }
